# Pre-commit Hooks

This project uses [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com) to run code quality checks
automatically before every `git commit`. No check needs to be run manually — they fire on
`git commit` and block the commit if any hook fails.

---

## Installation

```bash
pip install pre-commit
```

That's all — git is already configured to use `.githooks/` and the `pre-commit` wrapper
script is already in place. No `pre-commit install` needed.

To run all hooks manually on the entire codebase (without committing):

```bash
pre-commit run --all-files
```

To run a single hook by id:

```bash
pre-commit run <hook-id> --all-files
```

---

## Git integration

This project configures git to look for hooks in `.githooks/` instead of the default
`.git/hooks/`. This allows the hook scripts to be version-controlled and shared with
collaborators.

`git config --local core.hooksPath .githooks` is set **per-project** (written to
`.git/config`). It only affects this repository — every other repo on your machine
continues using whatever hooks path is configured globally.

### Hook execution flow

Here is exactly what happens when you run `git commit`:

1. **`.githooks/pre-commit` fires** — git calls this because `core.hooksPath = .githooks`.
   It runs `pre-commit run --hook-stage pre-commit` against your staged files, executing
   the tools selected during project setup ({{ optional_deps | join(", ") }}).
   If any check fails the commit is blocked here and nothing further runs.

2. **`.githooks/prepare-commit-msg` fires** — this script immediately `exec`s into
   `~/.copilot/hooks/prepare-commit-msg` (if present), which generates an AI commit
   message from the staged diff. From git's perspective it is one uninterrupted hook;
   the Copilot logic runs as part of it.

3. **Commit is written.**

4. **`.githooks/post-commit` fires** — delegates to `~/.copilot/hooks/post-commit`
   (auto-pushes the branch to remote).

On `git push`:

5. **`.githooks/pre-push` fires** — delegates to `~/.copilot/hooks/pre-push`
   (auto-creates a draft GitHub PR for the branch).

Git only ever calls `.githooks/<name>`. It has no knowledge of the Copilot hooks — the
delegation scripts are the bridge. Because they use `exec "$HOOK" "$@"` the Copilot hook
*replaces* the delegation process (same PID, same args, same exit code). Both sets of
behaviour run, in the right order, with no duplication.

### Hook scripts in `.githooks/`

| Script | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `pre-commit` | Runs pre-commit framework checks on staged files |
| `prepare-commit-msg` | Delegates to `~/.copilot/hooks/prepare-commit-msg` if present |
| `post-commit` | Delegates to `~/.copilot/hooks/post-commit` if present |
| `pre-push` | Delegates to `~/.copilot/hooks/pre-push` if present |

The three delegation hooks silently skip if the global Copilot hooks are not installed on
a collaborator's machine — they will never block a commit.

> **Why not `.git/hooks/`?**  If your machine has `core.hooksPath` set globally
> (e.g. to `~/.copilot/hooks/`), git ignores `.git/hooks/` entirely. A project-local
> `core.hooksPath` takes precedence and lets both the pre-commit framework and global
> hooks coexist cleanly.

### For new contributors

After cloning the repository, install pre-commit once:

```bash
pip install pre-commit
```

No `pre-commit install` is required — `.githooks/pre-commit` is already in place and git
is already configured to use `.githooks/` via the committed `.git/config` setting.

---

## Updating hook versions

Hook revisions are pinned in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`. To upgrade all hooks to their
latest versions:

```bash
pre-commit autoupdate
```

---

## Hygiene hooks (always active)

These run on every commit regardless of which optional tools you selected.

| Hook | What it does |
|------|--------------|
| `trailing-whitespace` | Removes trailing spaces and tabs from every line |
| `end-of-file-fixer` | Ensures every file ends with exactly one newline |
| `check-added-large-files` | Blocks commits that accidentally add large binary files |
| `check-merge-conflict` | Prevents committing files that still contain merge conflict markers (`<<<<<<`, `>>>>>>`) |
| `mixed-line-ending` | Normalises line endings — prevents CRLF/LF mixing |
| `check-ast` | Parses every `.py` file and errors if the syntax is invalid Python |
| `debug-statements` | Blocks commits that contain `pdb`, `ipdb`, `breakpoint()`, etc. |
| `check-yaml` | Validates YAML files are well-formed |
| `check-toml` | Validates TOML files (including `pyproject.toml`) |
{% if "detect-secrets" in optional_deps %}
| `detect-aws-credentials` | Scans for accidentally committed AWS key patterns |
{% endif %}

